FFmpeg-based wasm decoder (decoder.js + decoder.wasm)

This is the buildable source of the decoder that ships with the Audio Player
extension. The prebuilt binaries live in v2/data/player/decoder/ and are a
drop-in replacement target for the artifacts produced here.

Derived from https://github.com/chandler-stimson/decoder.js (0.1.2) with the
following changes:

  * batch output is buffered (the original issued one fwrite per 4-byte
    sample, which dominated the decode time of large files); the buffers
    count their fill per channel (a shared counter interleaved the channels
    with garbage gaps)
  * an incremental streaming API (decoder_open / decoder_pull /
    decoder_seek / decoder_close) fed through JS callbacks so that playback
    can start long before the whole file is downloaded and decoded;
    decoder_pull reports HAVE_PCM / NEED_INPUT / DONE / READ_ERROR
  * the streaming demuxer exports Duration and Start Time, and every
    decoded frame carries its presentation time so that the host can place
    a windowed decode exactly
  * decoder_seek converts the target into the time base of the audio stream
    (the original passed AV_TIME_BASE units to a stream-indexed seek and
    landed far off in most containers) and bails out of persistent demuxer
    read errors instead of spinning
  * the build enables libswresample: with --disable-all, configure drops
    every decoder whose dependencies are unmet, and the native Opus decoder
    depends on swresample (such files then failed at open with "the codec
    is not supported" while the container itself demuxed fine)
  * a use-after-free in the batch clean-up path was fixed

The batch entry point `decoder` keeps its 0.1.2 contract, so the binaries can
be swapped into the extension without any code change; the streaming API is
consumed by v2/data/player/decoder/worker.js.

Build (requires emsdk, git, make, pkg-config):

  bash emsdk.sh        # once; installs the pinned emsdk 3.1.61
  source <emsdk-dir>/emsdk/emsdk_env.sh
  cd dev/decoder && bash build.sh

The build compiles FFmpeg (pinned) and links the decoder; its output
(decoder.js, decoder.wasm) is produced in the build directory, verified
against the streaming contract with dev/tests/wasm-smoke.cjs and copied
over the checked-in binaries in v2/data/player/decoder/ (pass --no-copy to
keep them out of the extension directory). After a build that changes the
artifacts, bump versions.core in v2/data/player/decoder/wrapper.js.

The link step falls back to a binaryen-free build when the wasm-opt of the
emsdk cannot run on the host or is of a different version than the pinned
one (its import renaming then produces a wasm that the generated glue
cannot instantiate; the fallback is verified by the smoke test, not just
triggered by a crash): decoder.c is compiled at -O3 -msimd128 on its own
and the final link runs at -O0 so that emcc skips its binaryen stage. The
FFmpeg libraries are optimized either way, so only the whole-wasm post-link
passes are lost. This is what happens on a musl (Alpine) host: the emsdk
binaries are glibc builds that run under the gcompat layer, except the
bundled node (build.sh points EM_NODE_JS at the node of the host) and
wasm-opt (which segfaults on inputs of this size).

A GitHub workflow (.github/workflows/build-decoder.yml) builds the artifacts
automatically on changes to this directory. The emsdk of the workflow
container is pinned to the version the checked-in binaries were built with.

Node tests: dev/tests/ drives the built binaries through the streaming API,
the worker protocol, the wrapper and a real mp4 sample end to end, and the
windowed playback scheduler of the audio view:

  node dev/tests/wasm-smoke.cjs
  node dev/tests/worker-stream.cjs
  node dev/tests/worker-mp4.cjs            (downloads the Sintel trailer once)
  node dev/tests/worker-opus.cjs           (downloads a small WebM once; guards
                                            the codec set: Opus in Matroska)
  node dev/tests/wrapper.cjs
  node dev/tests/audio-view-scheduler.cjs
  node dev/tests/audio-view-integration.cjs (as above; needs the sample)
